<section class="example-section">
  {{#> important-callout}}
  This guide can be used in general to sign bitcoin core transactions between an online node and an offline node.
  {{/important-callout}}

  <p>Make sure to follow the <a href="../setup-devices/requirements.hbs" target="_blank">guide</a> to setup online and offline devices before you continue with this guide.</p>

  <h2>Sign transactions</h2>
  <p>The process is as follows:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>Input encrypted backup to obtain binary</li>
    <li>Input one-time pad to decrypt backup into a seedphrase</li>
    <li>Input passphrase to derive BIP39 wallet</li>
    <li>Run python script that bitcoin core private descriptors off BIP39</li>
    <li>Import private descriptors into bitcoin core</li>
    <li>Derive public descriptor and transfer it to online device</li>
    <li>Online device creates PSBT and transfer it to offline device</li>
    <li>Offline device signs PSBT and transfers it to online device</li>
    <li>Online device verifies and broadcasts signed transaction</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Super Base 58 (SB58)</h2>
  <p>This guide offers an interactive transfer via Super Base 58 (SB58) which is inspired on a format created by the bitcoin yeti guide. SB58 uses the NATO phonetic alphabet to encode base58 data. Each character in the base58 data is encoded as a word from the NATO phonetic alphabet. It ignores casing to improve UX for reading and typing. Since base58 does require casing it supports it with an additional 5-bit binary string where each of the first four bits signals whether that character is upper (1) or lowercase (0), the fifth bit does the same for the checksum word, and numbers ignore casing. Numbers are spelled out like 7 = seven.</p>
  <p>Then there are checksums. Each line contains 4 words and one binary casing string followed by a checksum word that comes from yeti. It is a position based checksum and there is only a single correct checksum word per 4 words. A single SB58 line looks like this <code>seven charlie tango bravo 01101 lima</code> which is base58 <code>7CTb</code> and the checksum is there to ensure everything else matches. Yeti uses casing instead of the binary map, but I think casing makes it much harder and slower to type because it requires more focus. Whereas typing everything lowercase and reading a 5-bit binary string is straight forward. With the UX help of the tool provided it results in very fast typing. Finally SB58 has a final checksum which is the last two bytes of the sha256 hash of all the lines above joined by newlines, written as a 5 digit decimal number. If you skipped a line or added one line or changed the order of lines the final checksum will not match.</p>
  <p>Why transfer with SB58 instead of QR codes? First, not all devices have cameras, and the ones that do don't always have good quality cameras. Second, introducing more dependencies to support creating and scanning QR codes is something I prefer to avoid. For bitcoin transactions the byte data size to transfer is below 1KB and most commonly transactions are only a few hundred bytes resulting in SB58 transfer speeds of less than 5 minutes. While it might sound like a lot, you would be surprised how long it takes to align cameras on two laptops to perform a scan, specially if they have low quality cameras like lots of old laptops do. Even if you prefer to use USB for transfering which is not recommended in this guide, it still takes a few minutes to insert a USB, mount it, transfer files, eject it, physically eject it, move it to the other device, mount it and transfer files. It actually ends up taking roughly the same time. If you are a fast typer you won't have any problems transfering with SB58.</p>
  <p>Try it on the <a href="../../super-base-58/index.html">Super Base 58</a> page, which turns any text into SB58 lines and back.</p>
